.github/workflows/*.lock.yml linguist-generated=true merge=ours

# --- GitHub linguist language-bar accuracy ---
# Sailfin (.sfn) has no upstream linguist grammar yet, so the compiler,
# runtime, and capsules (the actual codebase, all .sfn) contribute zero
# bytes to GitHub's language stats. Without the rules below the bar is
# computed entirely from the marketing site and dev tooling, which
# misrepresents the repo as a Shell/Astro/TypeScript project. These
# attributes exclude the non-representative surfaces so the bar reflects
# what's left honestly (rather than aliasing .sfn to an unrelated
# language, which would pollute GitHub language search). A real "Sailfin"
# entry requires an upstream github-linguist submission backed by broad
# repo adoption.

# Marketing / documentation site (Astro, CSS, config) — not the language.
site/** linguist-documentation

# Developer & build tooling — not the language.
tools/** linguist-vendored
scripts/** linguist-vendored
.claude/** linguist-vendored
.codex/** linguist-vendored
install.sh linguist-vendored

# --- Line endings ---
# `.sfn` and `.toml` check out LF on every host, overriding a contributor's
# `core.autocrlf=true` (the Git for Windows default).
#
# `sfn fmt` canonicalizes to LF, so a CRLF working tree makes `sfn fmt --check`
# report EVERY file as needing reformatting. That is the exact gate CI runs on
# `compiler/src/`, `compiler/capsules/`, and `runtime/`, so on Windows it is
# both unpassable and uninformative — it names every file and explains none of
# them (SFN-1106).
#
# The fmt gate is not the whole cost. Windows-only runtime source is guarded by
# static `contains` assertions against whole-file text, because
# `runtime/sfn/platform/process_windows.sfn` is selected only by the runtime
# manifest's Windows target table and no Linux or macOS leg compiles it. Several
# of those pins span a newline in order to guard placement rather than presence,
# and no CRLF checkout can satisfy any of them — measured, 2 of the 3 failures
# in `compiler/tests/e2e/process_run_stdio_passthrough_test.sfn` on a native
# Windows host were line-ending artifacts alone.
#
# `.toml` is here for a second, independent reason: the compiler reads capsule
# manifests as bytes, and tests anchor on their exact text.
# `compiler/tests/e2e/cross_windows_runtime_modules_test.sfn` asserts a
# `"sfn-sources-add = [\n    ..."` substring against `runtime/capsule.toml`,
# which no CRLF checkout can satisfy.
#
# A fresh clone picks this up automatically. An existing Windows clone does NOT,
# and `git add --renormalize .` will not fix it: the committed blobs are already
# LF (`git ls-files --eol` reports `i/lf`), so there is nothing to renormalize,
# and because `text eol=lf` normalizes CRLF->LF on the way in, git considers a
# CRLF working tree clean — which makes `git checkout` and
# `git checkout-index -f` no-ops on it too. The CRs have to come off the working
# tree directly (`dos2unix`, an editor, or):
#
#   git ls-files --eol -- '*.sfn' '*.toml' | grep 'w/crlf' | sed 's/^.*\t//' \
#     | while IFS= read -r f; do sed -i 's/\r$//' "$f"; done
#
# `git diff` stays empty afterwards, since the content already matched the index.
*.sfn text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf

# The same reasoning reaches past source, to two groups the `.sfn`/`.toml`
# rules above left uncovered (SFN-1115). Measured on a native Windows host,
# `git ls-files --eol` reported 573 tracked files checked out `w/crlf` with
# `attr/` empty — nothing governing them.
#
# Group 1 — golden artifacts a test compares BYTE-EXACTLY. A CRLF checkout
# makes the comparison unsatisfiable, and the failure names only a line
# number, so it reads as a logic bug rather than a checkout artifact.
# `compiler/tests/unit/lowering_trace_sink_test.sfn:79` asserts captured
# stderr equals `fixtures/lowering_trace_success.stderr`; the compiler emits
# LF (the `_setmode` binary-mode fix, SFN-1114) while the fixture arrived
# CRLF, so it could never match.
*.stderr text eol=lf
*.snap text eol=lf
*.ll text eol=lf
*.sfn-asm text eol=lf
# `.sh` and `.yml` join them for the same reason and one more: they are read
# back as text and pinned by `\n`-joined substrings.
# `aarch64_binfmt_probe_test.sfn:95` asserts against
# `scripts/bootstrap-aarch64-linux.sh`, and `:100` asserts a two-line
# `SAILFIN_BOOTSTRAP=off \\\n            SAILFIN_LINKER=lld` substring against
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. A substring spanning a newline is the shape a
# CRLF checkout always breaks, and a single-line one is the shape it usually
# survives — which is why this class shows up as an unreproducible subset of a
# file's tests rather than the whole file.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Group 2 — key and digest material, where a translated byte is not a
# cosmetic difference but a verification failure. `bootstrap.toml [verify]`
# runs `signed-sha256sums` over `SHA256SUMS` + `SHA256SUMS.sig`, and the
# release-signing public keys are read as bytes.
#
# Every one of these is a TEXT encoding here, verified rather than assumed:
# the `.sig` fixtures are 128 hex characters plus a line ending (129 bytes in
# the index, 130 in a CRLF working tree), not the 64 raw bytes a detached
# Ed25519 signature is often stored as. So `eol=lf` is both correct and
# preferable to `-text`: it stops the checkout conversion AND normalizes on
# the way in, whereas `-text` would let a CRLF `.sig` committed from a
# Windows host survive into the index. A raw-binary signature added later
# would need `-text` instead.
*.sig text eol=lf
*.pem text eol=lf
*.hex text eol=lf
SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
*_SHA256SUMS text eol=lf
